{"id": "40c496c8-edb3-4a63-992b-c3ec9189106d", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T11:02:22.720608", "source": "user", "parent_id": "dbaff6d8-afb3-4c57-b7d0-fbce715ae75e", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{"researcher_request": "This is a design-only task. The question is: how does the **length of an adversarial suffix** in token-level jailbreak attacks relate to Attack Success Rate (ASR) on LLMs, and is there a saturation point where longer suffixes stop helping? Please do not run any experiments; produce an experiment design with rationale, controls, analysis plan, and feasibility. I need three things in this first pass only — do NOT yet write the full design document. Items expected below are assumed unless you justify otherwise; unknowns are honest gaps in the brief, not facts to invent. Your one partition point will be after I review your approach, then you write the final deliverable. With that set, please return: **1. Repository inspection.** Briefly check /workspace (use `python3` to list files, peek at any subfolders, check whether any Python/venv, dependencies, or model weights already exist). If `track.md` or other source files are present, summarize what they constrain. Do not assume compute, network, or pretrained weights are available — this environment is bubblewrap, network disabled, no GPU. **2. Proposed approach (outline only).** Address each of the following explicitly, and mark anything that is an *assumption* (not source-supported) with [ASSUMPTION]:   a. **Attack family.** Which token-level jailbreak method(s) will be the suffix source? Candidates include GCG, AutoDAN, BEAST-style random/beam search, or genetic-algorithm variants. Justify the choice against the question (suffix-length vs ASR).   b. **Suffix-length variable.** Concrete length values (in *tokens*, not characters) you would sweep — e.g., a range from a baseline of 0 up to some upper bound, and the resolution/granularity of the sweep needed to detect a saturation point.   c. **Controlled confounders.** Fix or report: target model(s) and access mode (white-box gradients vs transfer/black-box only), prompt/behavior source dataset (e.g., AdvBench subset, HarmBench, JailbreakBench), decoding settings, evaluation judge (string-match, GPT-judge, refusal classifier), number of seeds/initializations (for stochastic attacks), truncation behavior when suffix is too long for the context window.   d. **ASR definition.** Per-attempt vs per-behavior, and whether partial-suffix results are redacted/prefixed.   e. **Analysis plan.** How you would (i) plot ASR vs suffix length, (ii) test for a saturation point (e.g., piecewise/logistic fit, marginal-gain threshold, breakpoint regression), (iii) report variance across seeds and behaviors, and (iv) handle length measured in tokens vs BPE-merge efficiency (different tokenizers compress the suffix differently — this can itself drive saturation).   f. **Feasibility.** Given the runtime budget (no GPU, no network, dependencies not preinstalled), state honestly what is *designable and explainable on paper* vs what would only be feasible with external compute you do not have.   **3. Assumptions and uncertainties to resolve before design finalization.** List every [ASSUMPTION] you made (target model size, attack algorithm, dataset size, number of seeds, token-count upper bound, judge choice, etc.), and flag which the user/researcher must confirm vs which you will commit to in the design. Also flag whether multiple tokenizers are in/out of scope — the saturation signal may be tokenizer-dependent. Keep the response tight: bullet form is fine. I will either approve the approach and ask you to produce the final design, or push back on specific items.", "required_handoff_stage": "plan", "approach_approved": false, "public_task": {"question": "How does the **length of adversarial suffix** used in token-level jailbreak attacks affect the **Attack Success Rate (ASR)** of LLMs? 