{"id": "fd1ecb29-4d3c-4c90-a8b7-3e3bb2d1cfdc", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T11:05:31.679819", "source": "user", "parent_id": "959cb558-7ece-48c6-980f-0232e104df0a", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{"researcher_request": "Approach approved with six sharpening notes to fold into the final deliverable. The overall structure (GCG primary, BEAST secondary, token sweep {0,1,2,5,10,20,50,100,200,500}, three linear + 2 instruction-tuned models, AdvBench, Llama-Guard judges, no live run, design + literature-anchored forecast) is correct for this design-only contract and environmentally honest given bubblewrap/no-GPU/no-network. Open items below must be resolved inside the final design document, not by re-asking me: commit to specific numbers so the protocol is reproducible on paper. Note 1 — Promote the prefix-of-suffix cell from secondary to a co-equal analysis arm. The question 'is there a saturation point beyond which longer suffixes no longer improve ASR' has two distinct interpretations: (a) trained-at-L, evaluated-at-L (the primary curve), and (b) trained-at-L*=large, evaluated-at-prefix-ℓ (does the attack's effectiveness live in the first ℓ tokens of an optimized longer suffix?). (b) is the strongest direct test of saturation and arguably the most informative endpoint. State in the design that the primary saturation claim must be supported by BOTH arms; if only one arm saturates, that is itself a finding worth reporting. Note 2 — Define the saturation criterion crisply and commit numbers. Use a composite test for 'saturated': (i) marginal gain test: |ΔASR(L_{k+1}, L_k)| < τ for two consecutive doublings of L on the primary arm, with the bootstrap 95% CI on ΔASR including zero across behaviors×seeds; commit τ = 0.02 absolute ASR (i.e., <2 percentage points). (ii) broken-stick regression with at most one breakpoint L* and bootstrap CI over behaviors×seeds (require CI width ≤ 0.5·L_lower_bound to call L* identified). (iii) sigmoid fit ASR(L) = a + (b−a)/(1+exp(−k(L−L_0))) and report L_50 (length at half of asymptotic gain) with parameter CI. Require (i) AND [(ii) OR (iii)] to declare saturation at L*. Note 3 — Commit a concrete GCG optimization budget per L so per-token effort is bounded and the reported ASR-vs-L curve is not confounded with optimization effort. Recommended: 500 steps for L∈{1,2,5,10,20}; 2500 steps for L∈{50,100}; 5000 steps for L∈{200,500}. State this protocol-level commitment in the design, and note that budgets for L>500 are NOT part of the curve (extrapolation is a discussion paragraph, not a measurement). Note 4 — Scope tokenizer-robustness as a small targeted check, not a full second sweep. Run the full sweep under the primary tokenizer (Llama-2 SP); then rerun ONLY at L∈{0, 50, 500} under one additional tokenizer (GPT-2 BPE) on one model (Llama-2-7B-chat). Report merge-ratio (chars/token) and effective-character-length ASR alongside token-length ASR so saturation can be discussed independently of tokenizer compression. Note 5 — The final deliverable must include a 'saturation summary card' with exactly three numbers per model: (a) early steepness = ASR(L=50) − ASR(L=0); (b) asymptotic gain = ASR(L=L_max) − ASR(L=0); (c) L* with CI from broken-stick, plus a one-line verbal call of 'saturated' / 'not saturated' / 'partially saturated'. Note 6 — Concrete external-compute cap to anchor feasibility. State that the primary white-box GCG arm is bounded at ≤24 GPU-hours per model on an A100 (≈5 seeds × 25 behaviors × median L ≈ 200 × modest per-step cost); the prefix-of-suffix arm adds ≤8 GPU-hours per model; the BEAST secondary arm adds ≤8 GPU-hours per model; the tokenizer-robustness check adds ≤4 GPU-hours. Total cap: ≤48 GPU-hours per model for primary, ≤48 GPU-hours per model for secondary/robustness combined. If a reviewer later asks for more L values, give the cost table, not a commitment to run. Also acknowledge in the design that the judge itself (Llama-Guard-3-8B primary, Llama-Guard-2-8B fallback) is another 8B model that must be loaded for evaluation — log this as a budget line and state that the judge model is NOT used for optimization. Proceed to the final design document incorporating all six notes. Structure it as: (1) Objective & hypotheses, (2) Variables and factors, (3) Models & attack-family selection with rationale, (4) Suffix-length protocol with explicit per-L optimization budget, (5) Judge selection & ASR definitions (per-behavior and per-attempt), (6) Primary + prefix-of-suffix + BEAST-secondary + tokenizer-robustness arms, (7) Analysis plan with the three saturation tests and the 3-number summary card, (8) External-compute feasibility cap with cost table, (9) Literature-anchored forecast citing Zou 2023 GCG, Andriushchenko 2024, Sadasivan 2024 BEAST, HarmBench, JailbreakBench, Mazeika 2024 (for judges), with explicit 'expected direction' and 'magnitude range' expectations flagged as forecasts not measurements, (10) Threats to validity & how the design mitigates each, (11) Limitations of a design-only deliverable. Use section numbers. Aim for a tight, self-contained document; do not run anything. Do not pad. When complete, hand off for my final result review.", "required_handoff_stage": "result", "approach_approved": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "kind": "MessageEvent", "source_event_index": 14}
